A minimal budget check using `bundlesize`-style config, wired to fail the build:

```json
// bundlesize.config.json
{
  "files": [
    {
      "path": "./dist/main.*.js",
      "maxSize": "170 kB",
      "compression": "gzip"
    },
    {
      "path": "./dist/checkout.*.js",
      "maxSize": "90 kB",
      "compression": "gzip"
    }
  ]
}
```

```yaml
# .github/workflows/ci.yml (excerpt)
- name: Check performance budget
  run: npx bundlesize
  # non-zero exit fails the workflow, blocking merge
```

The budget is per-chunk, not one global number — `main` (loaded on every route) gets a tight budget, while a route-specific chunk like `checkout` gets its own, separately-tracked limit. A single global budget hides which specific route actually regressed.

The main way to stay under a bundle budget without cutting features is **code-splitting**: instead of shipping every route's code in one bundle, split it so a user visiting the home page never downloads checkout's code until they navigate there.

```javascript
// Route-based split: this import only fetches when /checkout is visited
const CheckoutPage = React.lazy(() => import('./routes/CheckoutPage'));
```

The budget conversation and the code-splitting conversation are the same conversation: a budget without a splitting strategy just pressures teams to inline everything into fewer, larger files to "simplify," which makes the budget harder to hit, not easier. The next slide compares the two common splitting granularities.
